  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below average temperatures and mainly dry\nconditions are expected into the weekend. \n\nCanadian high pressure will build from the upper GL today to \nNE by Fri night. The CAA on N'ly winds will support below \nnormal highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s. Humidity will also \nbe low, with Tds observed in the 40s today across the northern \nhalf of the CWA. Tonight will be the coolest for most areas with\ncalm winds and mostly clear skies. Any rural areas could drop \ninto the 50s, while the urban and bayshore remain in the mid \n60s. Would not be surprised if colder valleys dropped into upper\n40s. Fri will feature more mid and high level cloud cover.\n\nAs the high becomes positioned to the NE, an inverted trough \nwill develop along the Appalachian Mountains. This will help \ndraw some moisture back nwrd from the stalled frontal zone \nacross the SE-US. S/WVs in the NW flow could combine with the \nconvergence in the surface trough to produce a few showers at \ntimes Fri afternoon through Sat night. This rain is most \nprobable west of I-81, but a few showers across the srn tier \ncan't be ruled out depending on how the stationary front to our \nS buckles. While low temps will start creeping up in response \nto higher Tds, Sat high temps should remain below normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Returning to a summer-like pattern next week.\n\nUL troffing will persist through much of next week over the NE \nUS. S/WVs will move through the broad NW H5 flow with some \nguidance developing a cut-off low over NE towards the middle of \nnext week. Regardless of UL evolution, a return to a summer-like\npattern is favored with a notable increase in PWs/sfc RH. Heat \nbuilds gradually as well, but nothing near headline levels at \nthis time. The increase in heat and moisture will increase \ninstability and t-storm chances. Rain chances increase gradually\neach day through Tue (when there is some consensus a cdfnt could\napproach), but admittedly varies which day is highest between \nguidance. ML guidance highlights Tue as the highest day for SVR \nwx, though low probs exist almost daily.",
